From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:40:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] drm/i915: the policy of Dolby Vision through a
 DisplayPort to HDMI converter

Many boards (the HDMI socket of Intel NUCs and mini PCs of the 11th
generation and later) have a DisplayPort to HDMI protocol converter
between the DDI port and the socket. For i915 such a port is a
DisplayPort connector, not an HDMI one, and the Dolby Vision tunnel
(RGB at 8 bits as a byte tunnel, signalled by the Dolby vendor
InfoFrame) has been refused there: intel_dv_lab_native_hdmi() takes
HDMI ports only.

What a converter does with the bytes is not in a standard: it turns
DisplayPort InfoFrame SDPs into HDMI InfoFrames in its own way, and one
that does not carry the Dolby vendor InfoFrame leaves the display
without Dolby Vision, or without a picture. So this adds the rule, not
yet its use (the next patch uses it), with the default that nothing
changes:

- intel_dv_pcon_policy.h, with no hardware access in it (a host test
  runs it): the list of boards where the tunnel was seen working
  through the converter (the DMI board vendor and name; one board
  today, with where it was seen), the test of a port that can carry it
  at all (display version 11 or later, enable_dv_lab, dv_pcon not 0,
  not eDP, not an LSPCON of the VBT, HDMI in the VBT), and the verdict
  that the connector will give user space: none, unlisted, listed,
  forced.
- the module parameter dv_pcon: -1 (the default) lets the list of
  boards decide, 0 turns it off, 1 takes every converter as verified
  (experimental).
- intel_dv_lab_validate() and intel_dv_lab_refused() take a converter
  that the policy takes where they took native HDMI only: the rest of
  the tests (RGB, 8 bits, full range, no HDR metadata, the mode, the
  sink) are the same for both.

The idea, the mechanism and the first board are from YBLOD (github.com/
dangerouslaser/libreelec-yblod, its linux-9930 "Standard Dolby Vision
through an onboard DP-to-HDMI protocol converter", verified there on an
INUSUAL INUC 11i5FL with a Sony Bravia 7 at 4K23.976 and 4K59.94). The
code is written again for the checks and the readout of this tree.

Not tried on hardware. Host test: the list, the candidate, the verdict
and the validation, in both directions.
---
 .../drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c   |   5 +
 .../drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h   |   1 +
 .../drm/i915/display/intel_dv_lab_policy.h    |   6 +-
 .../drm/i915/display/intel_dv_pcon_policy.h   | 155 ++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 165 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dv_pcon_policy.h

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
index a07bc3f21..08224a62a 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
@@ -36,6 +36,11 @@ intel_display_param_named_unsafe(dmc_firmware_path, charp, 0400,
 	"DMC firmware path to use instead of the default one. "
 	"Use /dev/null to disable DMC and runtime PM.");
 
+intel_display_param_named(dv_pcon, int, 0400,
+	"Standard Dolby Vision through the DisplayPort to HDMI converter of a board (display version 11 and later): "
+	"-1=boards where it was seen working only [default], 0=off, 1=every converter "
+	"(experimental: a converter that does not carry the Dolby vendor InfoFrame shows no Dolby Vision, or no picture).");
+
 intel_display_param_named_unsafe(enable_dv_lab, bool, 0400,
 	"Experimental Standard-DV RGB8 tunnel, display9+ native HDMI (default: on). "
 	"Requires independently validated packed pixels and HDMI frame metadata.");
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h
index 5e422975a..0c65e964d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@ struct drm_printer;
  */
 #define INTEL_DISPLAY_PARAMS_FOR_EACH(param) \
